tight  7mu < W <= 8mu:  1500 arrangements, 1696 runners with max > 7*min
   by speed-rank r_1..r_8 :   857      0      0      0      0      0      0    839
   at the two extremes    : 1696 / 1696  (100.0%)

general integer speeds:  1500 arrangements, 8480 runners with max > 7*min
   by speed-rank r_1..r_8 :   898   1190   1124   1031   1014   1094   1202    927
   at the two extremes    : 1825 / 8480  (21.5%)

general real speeds:  1500 arrangements, 8537 runners with max > 7*min
   by speed-rank r_1..r_8 :   885   1184   1100   1073   1070   1123   1195    907
   at the two extremes    : 1792 / 8537  (21.0%)

READING.
  Tight regime: 100% of the hard difference sets sit at the two extremes.
  General arrangements: only ~21% do -- and if rank were irrelevant the figure
  would be 25%, so the extremes are slightly UNDER-represented, not over. Ranks 2
  and 7 carry the most (1190, 1202) against 898 and 927 at the extremes.

  So 'the endpoints r_1, r_8 are where the hard ds arise' is a fact about the TIGHT
  regime only, and I stated it generally. Section 4.6 already says why: 'Beyond
  W = 8*mu the short gap moves into the interior as runners cross.' Once it has
  migrated, every rank carries hard sets and rank is exactly the convention the
  owner called it.

  What survives: ExistenceAt8 depends on ds alone, so rank was never a property of
  the object in the first place -- which is the same reason 'interior runner' could
  not extend its coverage.
